DIAGNOSTIC: read the CSIPHY registers back after configuring it

WHY
---
Everything else has already been ruled out WITH MEASUREMENT on the device:

  - the sensor emits (its frame counter advances and the PHY's activity rate
    follows the frame rate when it is lowered),
  - the CSIPHY hears it (stopping the sensor over I2C takes its interrupts to
    zero),
  - the five lanes, clock included, come out of idle,
  - the clocks are all on and at their rate (looked at WHILE it transmits),
  - the routing is the right one (PHY_NUM_SEL swept from 0 to 7),
  - the link speed does not matter (swept from 2393 to 307 Mbps per lane),
  - nor does the settle count (swept from 10 to 58, twice),
  - the sensor firmware is properly loaded (the 244 values of its internal RAM
    read back one by one: 244 out of 244 match),
  - and the CSID receiver DOES know how to report: with its pattern generator it
    marks SOT and EOT on all four lanes.

And even so the PHY does not hand the CSID a single symbol.

The silliest thing of all is left to check: that the PHY's writes land. A block
that is only half powered, a wrong offset or a register that ignores what is
written to it show up here and nowhere else. And it cannot be looked at from
userspace: with CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM, reads through /dev/mem silently return
zeros.

WHAT IT DOES
------------
After writing the lane table, it walks it again reading each register and warns
about the ones that do not return what was written. It sits behind the
readback_registers parameter because it is 115 reads for every transmission start.

This does NOT fix anything: it is for looking.
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c	2026-08-17 00:12:20.920071431 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c	2026-08-17 00:12:40.484056759 +0200
@@ -1057,6 +1057,11 @@
  * reboot, which is expensive on this phone: one boot in four hangs
  * arranques.
  */
+static int readback_registers;
+module_param(readback_registers, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(readback_registers,
+	"1 = read the CSIPHY registers back after configuring it and report the ones that do not match.");
+
 static int unmask;
 module_param(unmask, int, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(unmask,
@@ -1179,6 +1184,45 @@
 		if (r->delay_us)
 			udelay(r->delay_us);
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * DIAGNOSTIC: read back what has just been written.
+	 *
+	 * Everything else is already ruled out by measurement -the sensor transmits, the PHY
+	 * hears it, the clocks are set, the routing is the right one and the
+	 * CSID receiver DOES know how to report when something arrives, checked
+	 * with its pattern generator-. And even so the PHY does not deliver it a single
+	 * simbolo.
+	 *
+	 * The silliest thing is left to check: that the writes land. A
+	 * half-powered block, a wrong offset or a register that
+	 * ignores what is written to it show up here and nowhere else.
+	 *
+	 * It sits behind a parameter because it is 115 reads per start of
+	 * transmission and they are not needed all the time.
+	 */
+	if (!readback_registers)
+		return;
+
+	r = csiphy->regs->lane_regs;
+	for (i = 0; i < array_size; i++, r++) {
+		u32 leido;
+
+		if (r->csiphy_param_type == CSIPHY_SKEW_CAL ||
+		    r->csiphy_param_type == CSIPHY_DNP_PARAMS)
+			continue;
+
+		val = r->csiphy_param_type == CSIPHY_SETTLE_CNT_LOWER_BYTE ?
+			(settle_cnt & 0xff) : r->reg_data;
+
+		leido = readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + r->reg_addr);
+		if (leido != val)
+			dev_info(csiphy->camss->dev,
+				 "csiphy%d 0x%04x: escrito 0x%02x, leido 0x%08x\n",
+				 csiphy->id, r->reg_addr, val, leido);
+	}
+	dev_info(csiphy->camss->dev, "csiphy%d: relectura terminada\n",
+		 csiphy->id);
 }
 
 static u8 csiphy_get_lane_mask(struct csiphy_lanes_cfg *lane_cfg)
